  annotation = {中文辅助译名：具有自适应特征和样本权重及平衡约束的全局-局部数据聚类框架},
  abstract = {Clustering remains a hot research topic in machine learning and data mining communities. The underlying data similarity serves as the foundation of clustering data into respective groups. However, since different features have different discriminative capabilities and different samples might correspond to different importance levels, it is inaccurate to capture the data similarity if we ignore any of them in real applications. By taking both into consideration, we propose an adaptive feature and sample weighting (AFSW) scheme to quantitatively measure the importance in the least square regression sense, serving as a global clustering. As a local complementary, a graph is utilized to facilitate cluster indicator matrix learning, leading to a global-local (GL) clustering framework. As a secondary contribution, an entropy-based balanced constraint is introduced to enforce the cluster sizes to be close, which is a reasonable assumption if we are clueless to a certain given data set. In concrete model formulation, two strategies respectively based on self-paced learning and exponential probability coefficient are designed to determine the sample importance. The formulated model, termed GLAFSW, is optimized efficiently within an alternating direction framework. Experiments are performed on both synthetic and benchmark data sets from diverse aspects. By comparing the proposed framework against the state-of-the-arts, the results verified that it achieved competitive performance in cl},
}

