  abstract = {EEG-based speech imagery provides an intuitive communication paradigm for brain-computer interfaces (BCIs). However, traditional filtering based on empirical prior knowledge systematically discards valuable marginal-band information. Furthermore, the inherent non-linearity and multi-channel nature of EEG signals restrict the representational capacity during spatio-temporal feature extraction. In this letter, we propose LBF-CTA-Net, an end-to-end framework coupling adaptive frequency selection with spatio-temporal attention for decoding imagined speech intentions. Specifically, a Learnable Bandpass Filtering (LBF) module identifies optimal frequency ranges directly from subject-dependent EEG data. A convolutional network and a serial Channel-Temporal Attention (CTA) mechanism then process these features, facilitating the precise modeling of complex spatio-temporal dependencies. Evaluations on two public speech imagery EEG datasets yield superior classification accuracy to state-of-the-art baselines. Additionally, the learned filter distributions provide valuable neurophysiological interpretability by revealing optimal frequency ranges across the subject cohort.},
